[38;5;231m⏺[39m That answer changes the classification, so I'm stepping the path up before going further.

  [1mUpgrading to the architectural path.[0m "Identifies the actual person/account," "works across the app," "persists," and
  "other forms will need it later" describe an identity/session layer this repo doesn't have — a shared store, a place
  the ID lives between page loads, and consumers that don't exist yet. That's not a parameter added to one stub; it's a
  new subsystem that [38;5;153mlogin()[39m happens to be the entry point for. The brainstorming ratchet only goes one way, so we'll do
  questions → approaches → design → spec, and I won't write code until you've approved a spec.

  One flag up front, since it shapes the design rather than following from it: an account identifier persisted in the
  browser is readable and editable by anyone with devtools. It can be used to [3mlabel[0m things (logs, form prefill,
  analytics) but must never be treated as proof of who someone is — the server has to re-verify on every request that
  matters. I'll design to that line explicitly.

  First question, and it's the one everything else hangs off:

  [1mWhere does the authoritative ID come from today?[0m Right now [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m is a stub — [38;5;153mlogin()[39m never makes a request; it
  just returns [38;5;153m{ success: true, user: username }[39m. A persistent account identity has to originate somewhere trustworthy,
  and whether that source exists yet decides whether we're building the real thing or a seam with a fake behind it.
